my passion is a direct result of all of  the pain and all of the struggle and all  of the failures  I wouldn't change a thing growing up in  a pretty high achieving family one of  the things that was always emphasized  was to not spend more than you make and  that you're completely in control of  your circumstances I was the heavy kid  in a pretty fit active family and my mom  wasn't really okay with that and now she  admits that she felt like my weight  problem was a reflection on her as a  parent so there was a lot of talk about  you know so many talents and gifts  imagine what you could do if you just  lost that weight I wanted to be a high  achiever in every regard not just  financially but with my health in fact  so much so that I walked away from a  full scholarship in Latin and Greek to  study nutrition as an obese teenager  because I wanted more than anything to  tackle it and I was over 300 pounds I  wasn't thinking about what it would look  like when it came time to repay that  debt and so when I got married and I  realized between my husband's flight  school and my student loans we have one  hundred and thirty thousand dollars in  debt and we didn't get to do with our  money what we wanted to we had to pay  that student loan debt and I just  decided I want more freedom than that I  am a big reader I'm always taking on new  books and when I picked up financial  piece it really taught me how to stay in  the moment all I had to worry about was  today we had paid off all of our debt  that was great we owned a home that was  great I had a job that I was really good  at but why is it then I can achieve all  these other things and I can have total  tunnel vision and commitment to a goal  but not be able to achieve the weight  loss that I wanted and I decided it was  time to unlearn everything I learned in  school and transform this because I have  what it takes and now's the time to do  it  for me taking control of my finances was  really a turning point to allow me to  take control of everything in my world I  really wanted to take a common-sense  approach to these changes this time  around what I came to learn and  understand cuz I'm a big reader and  researcher is that the idea that all we  need to do is eat less and move more is  totally flawed all of a sudden when I  started taking kind of a common-sense  approach to what my body needed and  wanted it was so much easier than it had  ever been after I had lost about 130  pounds so many people were saying what  did you do how did you do this what was  the lightbulb moment how do you stay  motivated and I was like you know what  this is so pervasive and it's not just a  health issue it's a quality of life  issue and so I decided to launch final  potential and use my story to help other  people create for themselves the really  common sense approach to natural fat  loss hey everybody welcome back to the  primal potential podcast  I'm your host Elizabeth bendin and I'm  so glad that you've taken time out of  your day to hang out with me and talk  about what is holding you back and I  know for so many of my clients when they  start to control their mindset they're  better able to make great food choices I  have to remind myself all the time to  not get out in front not worry about  what I'm gonna do tonight or what I'm  gonna do next week and so this is a  constant reminder for me what is my best  in this moment motivation isn't  something that just strikes you it's  actually something you choose to create  every day I have to consciously choose  my focus throughout the day all day  every day  I've graffiti'd my treadmill desk with  some of those phrases that motivate me I  have them on the wall to be relentlessly  positive when I look at it now I am  happy to know that my passion comes from  my pain  I see it as the raw material there like  the bricks to my building but I am  defined by what I do and that is a gift
 

